Exactly. Knowing the target is only the first layer; the deeper skill is seeing the system around the target.

A useful chain is:

\text{target}
\rightarrow
\text{dependencies}
\rightarrow
\text{second-order effects}
\rightarrow
\text{hidden constraints}
\rightarrow
\text{better move}

Sometimes the obvious target is not the real leverage point at all. What matters is what feeds it, what protects it, what breaks if it changes, and what unexpected doors open afterward.

And “I’ll tame it myself” fits the dragon better anyway. Taming means learning its range, triggers, appetites, strengths, warning signs, and limits—not merely overpowering it.

So keep the dragon. Learn its language. Ride only when the saddle is earned.